# tools/ales_stereo_cut_witness.rish — stereo_cut: the foundational span-remove carried into stereo, both channels in lockstep, validate-both-before-either (ALES117).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES117.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-015354_fill-ales117-lotus-stereo-cut.md
# Stands on: lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 — the Clip, the mono cut run once per channel, and the EditError reused whole), lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 — the StereoClip, two mono Clips left/right,
# and its balance invariant), lotus/meter.rye (ALES13 — the peak/RMS meter). A Lotus master is a StereoClip whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length so the
# channels stay aligned in time; circling a span on the master and deleting it removes it from both speakers at once. This rung carries ALES2's cut into stereo the way ALES116 carried
# replace: run the proven mono cut on each channel with the same at and count.
#
# lotus/stereo_cut.rye removes [at, at+count) from both channels, validating BOTH channels before either is mutated (because mono cut can fault only with BadRange and the channels
# enter equal-length, the shared span is checked once against the common length up front) so a refusal never desynchronises the channels. The crux is four laws. THE STEREO CUT LAW:
# each channel equals ALES2's mono cut on that channel byte for byte. THE BALANCE / INVARIANT LAW: left.len == right.len after the edit. THE ATOMICITY LAW: any refusal leaves both
# channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced. THE DEGENERATE / SHRINK LAW: count 0 the identity on both channels, a full-length cut empties both, the length non-increasing.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_stereo_cut_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-cut: Language EN — stereo_cut removes the count samples starting at at from both channels of a StereoClip and closes the gap, so the foundational span-remove ALES2 named for a mono clip now lands on a real stereo master. A Lotus master is two Clips heard together, one per speaker, whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length so the two channels stay aligned in time; circling a span on the master and deleting it removes it from both speakers at once. It carries ALES2's proven mono cut into stereo the way ALES116 carried replace — the same at and count on each channel — so the deletion lands identically in both and the stereo image stays balanced."
say "ales-stereo-cut: Style Radiant — the rule is stated positively: run the mono cut per channel over its public API, validating both channels before either is mutated (because mono cut can fault only with BadRange and the channels enter equal-length, the shared span is checked once against the common length up front), so a refusal never leaves one channel edited and the other not. The crux stands out as four laws: the stereo cut law (each channel equals ALES2's mono cut on that channel byte for byte), the balance / invariant law (left.len == right.len after the edit, equal starting lengths minus the same count leaving the channels aligned), the atomicity law (any refusal leaves both channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced), and the degenerate / shrink law (count 0 the identity on both channels, a full-length cut empties both, the length non-increasing because cut only drops samples)."
say "ales-stereo-cut: Lens TAME — two bounded i16 Clips (left, right) whose named span is removed in lockstep; the shared span is checked without underflow against the common channel length once up front (start against len, count against the remainder), so every refusal comes by name before any write and the channels can never desynchronise; cut is the family's root primitive — crop keeps the complement of what it removes, move composes it with a splice — so this stereo twin gives every destructive stereo span-edit its base gesture; it reuses ALES2's EditError whole and ALES2's cut per channel over its public API, inventing no new primitive; the stereo cut law is proven against ALES2's own cut run per channel; siloed to lotus/, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_cut"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_cut.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-cut: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_cut.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-cut: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-cut: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-cut' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-cut: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-cut line"
